1
00:00:00.00 --> 00:00:06.92
Your agent just read a carrier note. Buried inside it is an instruction to exfiltrate your customer list.

2
00:00:05.97 --> 00:00:12.97
The agent cannot tell data from instruction. This is prompt injection, and every tool call is an attack surface.

3
00:00:15.40 --> 00:00:22.40
Current defenses are one bit: block the whole document, or pass it. Blocking destroys the data the agent needed.

4
00:00:22.76 --> 00:00:28.53
Passing ships the attack. SpanGuard takes the third option: strip the attack, keep the data.

5
00:00:30.88 --> 00:00:35.88
Here is the real guard, running the real models, on the real payload.

6
00:00:36.43 --> 00:00:43.43
The rule engine and the neural span model agree on the attack span. The order, the status, the total: untouched.

7
00:00:44.47 --> 00:00:51.47
And this is not an English-only trick. The corpus trains seven languages: English, German, Hungarian, French, Bangla, Arabic, Japanese.

8
00:01:06.29 --> 00:01:12.47
On the extended fixture, escape is zero point zero percent in every one of them, with document F one of one point zero.

9
00:01:12.97 --> 00:01:15.28
Version six adds two principled layers.

10
00:01:16.51 --> 00:01:23.51
A split-conformal bound calibrates the span threshold, so benign over-stripping is bounded by construction: zero point eight percent on five hundred new adversarial documents.

11
00:01:29.54 --> 00:01:36.54
And a task gate: give the guard the user's task, and canonical AgentDojo attacks drop from eighty to forty percent escape.

12
00:01:39.73 --> 00:01:42.04
But the real question is outcomes.

13
00:01:43.34 --> 00:01:49.88
Our agent-loop harness runs the cleaned output through a deterministic executor and scores utility and hijack together.

14
00:01:49.92 --> 00:01:56.92
On benign documents: utility one hundred percent. On canonical attacks: hijack success one point three percent, the best of four systems.

15
00:02:01.86 --> 00:02:07.24
A guard that strips everything wins escape and loses utility. We publish both columns.

16
00:02:08.76 --> 00:02:11.07
One corpus, one family, all open.

17
00:02:12.79 --> 00:02:19.79
A one hundred thirty-five million parameter multilingual span model, sub-million char experts, the one point seven million foundational model, and fm-v6.

18
00:02:25.04 --> 00:02:32.04
Every weight, every run record, every evaluation, on Hugging Face. Try it live in the demo Space, no install.

19
00:02:35.25 --> 00:02:39.86
Everything here is fictional data, committed, and re-verifiable. Tests green, Apache two.

20
00:02:43.54 --> 00:02:50.54
SpanGuard version six: strip the attack, keep the data, with a bound. GitHub, models, and the live demo are linked below.
